  "lineage": "Mississippi State Penitentiary (Parchman Farm) Black prison-labor work-song tradition documented by John A. Lomax and Alan Lomax for the Library of Congress in their 1933-onward Southern field recordings — captured the antiphonal call-and-response work-song tradition (the lead-singer call answered by the gang chorus, synchronized to the axe-stroke or hoe-stroke timing) that preserved African polyrhythmic vocal practice in the most brutal post-Reconstruction prison-labor context; figures Leadbelly (Huddie Ledbetter, recorded at Angola Louisiana State Penitentiary 1933 by John Lomax), the broader Parchman prison-farm corpus continuing through Alan Lomaxs 1959 and 1968 return recordings",
